  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea is to use a population-based optimization approach with adaptive dimensionality reduction to evolve candidate functions that minimize the maximum correlation integral. This differs from gradient-based methods by:\n\n- Using a diversity-aware search to avoid premature convergence\n- Implementing dimensionality reduction techniques to efficiently explore the function space\n- Applying noise-injection-based local refinement\n- Incorporating temperature-scheduling for exploration/exploitation balance\n\nThe bottleneck the parent solution faces (local optima) is addressed by:\n- Population-based exploration for global coverage\n- Intelligent dimensionality reduction to properly handle large grids\n- Hierarchical optimization: coarse grid for diversity, fine grid for refinement\n- Step-size adaptation based on function curvature\n\nThe important limitation is that we trade local refinement depth for population diversity. The method may find better global optima at the cost of deeper local refinement than the parent approach.\n\n2. Mathematical specifications\n\nThe optimization is over step functions with:\n- Grid size: chosen between 200 to 1000 points\n- Objective: minimize C_5(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Representation: piecewise constant functions represented by grid samples\n- Projection method: orthogonal projection onto feasible set (sum(h)=1/2) with bound constraints\n\nImplementation uses:\n- Squared Euclidean distance for diversity measures\n- Permutation-invariant Hamming distance for convergence checks\n- Non-dominated sorting for fitness assignment\n- Convex combination blending for interpolation\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n3.1 Population validation and initialization\n\ndef validate_parent(parent, n):\n    parent = np.array(parent, dtype=float)\n    # Scale to grid [0,2]\n    x = np.linspace(0,2,n)\n    x_parent = np.linspace(0,2,len(parent))\n    parent_grid = np.interp(x, x_parent, parent)\n    # Project to satisfy integral constraint\n    parent_grid = project_feasible(parent_grid, n)\n    return parent_grid\n\ndef init_population(base_grid_size=700, diversity=None):\n    if initial_h_values is not None:\n        start_vector = initial_h_values[:base_grid_size]\n        start_vector = project_feasible(start_vector, base_grid_size)\n        population = [start_vector]\n        # Sample diverse candidates from coarse grids\n        grid_sizes = [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700]\n        for gs in grid_sizes:\n            # Sample from kernel: combination of sinusoidal and random fixed mean\n            kernel = np.zeros(gs)\n            # Symmetric part\n            x = np.linspace(0,2,gs)\n            kernel += 0.3 * (0.5 + 0.2 * np.sin(np.pi * x) + 0.1 * np.sin(3*np.pi * x))\n            # Add mean varying with grid spacing\n            kernel += 0.2 * np.sin(2*np.pi*x * (gs/300))\n            kernel = np.clip(kernel, 0, 1)\n            kernel = project_feasible(kernel, gs)\n            kernel = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,base_grid_size), np.linspace(0,2,gs), kernel)\n            population.append(kernel)\n    else:\n        # Generate random population with kernel mixture\n        population = []\n        for i in range(20):\n            if i < len(diversity):\n                # Diversity-specific initialization\n                h_base = np.sin(2*np.pi * np.random.rand(len(diversity[i]))/diversity[i])\n                h_base = 0.5 * (1 + h_base) + 0.3 * np.random.randn(len(h_base))/10\n                h_base = np.clip(h_base, 0, 1)\n                h_final = project_feasible(h_base, 100)\n                h_final = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,base_grid_size), \\\n                np.linspace(0,2,100), h_final)\n                population.append(h_final)\n            else:\n                h_base = 0.5 + 0.2 * np.random.randn(base_grid_size)\n                h_base = np.clip(h_base, 0, 1)\n                population.append(project_feasible(h_base, base_grid_size))\n    # Remove duplicates and normalize\n    unique_indices = []\n    for i in range(len(population)):\n        if all(np.linalg.norm(population[i]-population[j]) > 2 for j in range(i)):\n            unique_indices.append(i)\n    population = [population[i] for i in unique_indices[:15]]\n    if len(population) < 15:\n        while len(population) < 15:\n            h_base = 0.5 + 0.2 * np.random.randn(base_grid_size)\n            h_base = np.clip(h_base, 0, 1)\n            if all(np.linalg.norm(h_base-population[i]) > 2 for i in range(len(population))):\n                population.append(project_feasible(h_base, base_grid_size))\n    return population\n\n3.2 Evaluation and selection\n\ndef fitness_function(h):\n    return -compute_c5(h, len(h))\n\ndef selection_by_diversity(population):\n    # Multi-objective NSGA-II style selection\n    # First compute dominance and crowding\n    obj_vals = [np.array([compute_c5(h, len(h)), np.sum(np.abs(h-0.5)**2)] for h in population]\n    # Sort by non-dominated sorting\n    fronts = []\n    # Binarization based on fitness and diversity\n    archive = []\n    for candidate in population:\n        dominates = []\n        for a in archive:\n            if all(c[i] <= a[i] for i in range(len(obj_vals[0]))) and not all(c[i] == a[i] for i in range(len(obj_vals[0]))):\n                dominates.append(a)\n        if dominates:\n            archive.append(candidate)\n    return archive\n\n3.3 Evolutionary operators\n\ndef crossover(parent1, parent2, p_crossover=0.9):\n    # Sobol sequence-based interpolation\n    if np.random.rand() < p_crossover:\n        ds = parent1 - parent2\n        # Blend using non-uniform interpolation\n        beta = np.random.beta(10, 10)\n        a = np.abs(np.random.randn(len(ds)))\n        delta = np.clip(beta * a * ds, -0.3, 0.3)\n        child = parent1 + delta\n    else:\n        child = 0.5 * (parent1 + parent2)\n    # Temperature-dependent variance addition\n    t = 0.05 * np.log10(max(1000, 1/(time.time()-t0)))\n    child += np.random.normal(0, t, len(child))\n    return project_feasible(child, len(parent1))\n\ndef mutation(child, p_mutation=0.2, t=0.05):\n    # Permutation mutation\n    ds = np.diff(np.where(np.random.permutation(len(child))[:50])[1:4])\n    low, high = 0.1, 0.9\n    perturb = (high - low) * np.exp(-np.power(ds, 2)/10)\n    perturb = perturb / np.sum(perturb)\n    delta = perturb * (np.random.rand(len(perturb)) - 0.5)\n    # Polynomial blend of controls\n    controls = [np.sin(2*np.pi*i/len(child)) for i in range(5)]\n    weights = np.exp(sum(controls)**2)\n    for i in range(len(child)):\n        if np.random.rand() < p_mutation:\n            child[i] += delta[i % len(delta)] * weights[i % len(weights)]\n    return project_feasible(child, len(child))\n\n3.4 Population adaptation\n\ndef adapt_grid(population_target=600):\n    # Grid size selection based on diversity\n    grid_logspace = np.logspace(2,4, num=7, base=2, endpoint=True)\n    grid_candidates = [int(x) for x in grid_logspace]\n    grid_candidates = [200] + grid_candidates\n    best_fitness = min([compute_c5(h, 200) for h in population])\n    best_grid = 200\n    # Dynamic grid expansion based on diversity requirements\n    if len(population) > 15 and best_fitness < 0.35:\n        grid_candidates.append(500)\n        grid_candidates.append(800)\n    elif len(population) < 10:\n        grid_candidates = [100, 200, 300]\n    # Fitness-based grid selection\n    grid_fitness = {}\n    for g in grid_candidates:\n        if g <= len(population[0]):\n            grid_fitness[g] = np.median([compute_c5(np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,len(h)), np.linspace(0,2,len(population[0])), h), g) \n                                        for h in population])\n    # Choose optimal grid\n    chosen_grid = None\n    best_score = float('inf')\n    for g in grid_candidates:\n        key = 'grid_' + str(g)\n        if key not in grid_fitness:\n            continue\n        if grid_fitness[key] < best_score:\n            best_score = grid_fitness[key]\n            chosen_grid = g\n    return chosen_grid\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\nThe default grid range is 100-1000 points. The initial population is initialized with:\n\n- Coarse grid parent (700 points)\n- Diverse collected solutions from different grids\n- Random samples heavily weighted toward low-frequency sinusoids\n\nKey configurable parameters:\n\n- Population size: [5, 20]\n- Crossover probability: [0.8, 1.0]\n- Mutation probability: [0.1, 0.3]\n- Grid sizing multiplier: [0.8, 1.2]\n\nEffective variation settings:\n\n{\n    \"champion_saving\": {\n        \"interval\": 10,\n        \"k\": 4   # top K champions\n    },\n    \"grid_adaptation\": {\n        \"initial_max\": 800,\n        \"increase_factor\": 1.1,\n        \"decrease_factor\": 0.9\n    },\n    \"diversity_maintenance\": {\n        \"methods\": [\"hamming\", \"fourier\"],\n        \"active\": [0.7, 0.3]\n    }\n}\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness\n\nTime budget distribution:\n\n- Initial evaluation (10%): grid sizing and initialization\n- Core evolution loop (60%): selection, crossover, mutation\n- Cooldown refinement (20%): local polishing\n- Candidate screening (10%): grid adaptation and dimensionality check\n\nwith main budget breakdown:\n\ndef budget_manager(elapsed, remaining):\n    if remaining < 5: return \"Halt\"\n    elif remaining < 20: return \"LocalRefine\"\n    elif remaining < 80: return \"GridAdaptation\"\n    else: return \"EvolutionSearch\"\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\ndef validate_and_return(candidates):\n    # Aggregate validation checks\n    for h in candidates:\n        L1 = np.sum(np.abs(h - 0.5))\n        L2 = np.std(h)\n        dx = 2.0 / len(h)\n        integral = np.sum(h) * dx\n        # Reject if outside safe bounds\n        if integral != 0.5 or L2 < 0.1 or L2 > 2.0:\n            continue\n        # Compute C5 bounds using original correlation\n    best = min([compute_c5(h, len(h)) for h in candidates])\n    return best, candidates[np.argmin([compute_c5(h) for h in candidates])]",
